1. What is Java?
Java is a high-level, object-oriented, platform-independent programming language.
High-level means it's easier for humans to read and write than machine code.
Object-oriented means programs are built using objects that represent real-world entities.
Platform-independent means you write Java code once and run it on any operating system that has a Java Virtual Machine (JVM).
That's why Java is often described as:
	Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)

2. What are JDK, JRE, and JVM?
JVM (Java Virtual Machine)
The JVM is responsible for running Java bytecode.
Its main responsibilities include:
	• Loading classes
	• Verifying bytecode
	• Managing memory
	• Running garbage collection
	• Executing the program
Without a JVM, a Java program cannot run.

JRE (Java Runtime Environment)
The JRE contains:
	• JVM
	• Core Java libraries
	• Runtime components
It's enough to run Java applications but not to develop them.

JDK (Java Development Kit)
The JDK includes:
	• JRE
	• JVM
	• Java compiler (javac)
	• Debugger
	• Development tools

If you're writing Java code, you need the JDK.
A simple way to remember the relationship is:
JDK
 ├── JRE
 │    └── JVM

3. What happens when you run a Java program?
Suppose you write:
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}

The flow is:
Hello.java
      ↓
javac (Compiler)
      ↓
Hello.class (Bytecode)
      ↓
JVM
      ↓
Machine Code
      ↓
Output on Screen
The compiler converts your source code into bytecode, and the JVM converts that bytecode into machine instructions for the operating system you're using.


NOTE:
The code is not compiled on every platform. It is compiled once into bytecode.
The JVM on each operating system executes that same bytecode.

DK stands for Java Development Kit. It is used for developing Java applications. It contains the JRE, JVM, compiler (javac), debugger, and other development tools.

JRE stands for Java Runtime Environment. It contains the JVM and the Java class libraries required to run Java applications. It does not include development tools like the compiler.
